rces that picture. The single tie poll is too old and too thinly sourced to offset the district's lean and the money gap.", "watch": "Fresh polling, a stronger-than-expected Democratic national environment, or fallout from scrutiny of Strada's home-inspection business could tighten the race.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Newest usable poll is 81 days old; polling uncertainty inflated.", "Open seat: Daniel Webster (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Florida's 11th District is an open seat after Republican Daniel Webster's retirement, and it has leaned Republican by about seven points in recent elections. Republican Joe Strada enters the general election with a decisive financial advantage, having raised $5.4 million to Democrat James Pericola's $197,000, along with $218,000 in outside support. Libertarian Ralph Groves is also on the ballot.\n\nEvery major race rater scores the seat for Republicans, most calling it solid or safe, and prediction markets give Pericola roughly a 7% chance of winning. The only public poll, a July survey with Democratic sponsorship, showed a 42-42 tie, but its age and sponsorship limit its weight. A favorable national environment for Democrats and recent scrutiny of Strada's home-inspection business are the main openings for an upset, but the seat remains clearly favored to stay in Republican hands.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.1071, "margin": -7.48}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.1312, "margin": -8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.1199, "margin": -8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.0703, "margin": -10.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.0839, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.0989, "margin": -8.5}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.1239, "margin": -8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.0883, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.1092, "margin": -8.5}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.0593, "margin": -10.0}, {"date": "2026-10-02", "p_dem": 0.1139, "margin": -